    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89202.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Welcome to the trade out podcast and thank you for spending Tuesdays with me. I decided to start the podcast with a virtue, a trait that I think is really important in life and is really important in our culture and that's fairness. It's hard to talk about fairness in one podcast, so I have broken it into three. I hope that you will listen to all three and more importantly, I hope that you will provide some feedback and tell me how you define fairness, where you rank it in the list of attributes that we need in our culture and what we can do to facilitate more of it in public discourse and in how we treat one another. And then we'll talk about how insidious both the reality and the perception of unfairness can be. In other words, not just what fairness is and why we value it, but what effect does unfairness have on the recipient? And then that will lead to our third conversation, which is how do we objectively test, judge, or calibrate fairness? Who is the arbiter of what is fair and what is not fair? But for now at least, let's just focus on fairness and the words we associate with fairness like justice and beauty and equality and even handedness, those are all words we would assign merit to, their aspirational words, their concepts to be sought, to be pursued, to be nurtured, to be perfected. But what do they mean? Does it matter whether we can define them or not? Is it enough that we have our own definitions of them or does there need to be some objective definition and test capable of being universally applied? Are those concepts of fairness and justice and equality and beauty and even handedness? Are they distinct or are they hopelessly intertwined? And before you conclude that it's okay for each of us to have our own definitions of fairness, is it really? Do you really mean that? Do you really believe that? How can I judge my fairness towards you or your fairness towards me if we're applying distinct definitions and attributes to the process? Mirror, mirror on the wall. Who's the fairest of them all? I think that was Snow White or Cinderella or Medea or some other show my sisters watched a lot. I just know I heard it a lot and paid absolutely no attention to it until recently. fairest one of all. I don't think she was asking who is the most impartial one of all who would make the best judge or Supreme Court justice. She was asking who is the most beautiful. But there's the word fair. You may think she was asking about who was lighter or pale or blonder, and you probably are right. But I think the words fairness and beauty and justice and equality are hopelessly intertwined. I think they are completely interconnected. And when you see fairness in action, it is beautiful. It has this radiant symmetry to it, this eternal evenness to it. I even love the spoken and written wordfare. It has a beautiful sound and a beautiful look. What about us as a culture, as a country? Do we like fairness when we see it? Someone who is willing to call it as he or she sees it, regardless of the crowd reaction that neutral detached arbiter, the person who treats friend and foe alike with due process, inequality in both intent and result, nearly everyone would laud fairness as an attribute, as a positive, something to be aspired to and clung to and administered. But what if you had to define it? What does it mean? I mean, we've all heard variations of the theme. You're not being fair. You're treating me unfairly. All I want is to be treated with some fairness. But what does the word mean? Does it mean equally? Is fairness tantamount to equality that we should treat similarly situated people similarly? Probably that's a good beginning definition until we run into the inevitable problem that finding distinguishing facts or circumstances really isn't that hard. So are two people ever really completely, similarly situated? I don't want you to assume two people are scheduled for an 8 a.m. job interview at a local coffee shop with a putative employer, and they're both late for the interview. I'm not an employment counselor, but I'm pretty sure showing up for a job interview late isn't good. Candidate one prepared for the interview, went to bed on time, set one alarm for 6 a.m. and another for 6 10 a.m. as a backup. But a thunderstorm comes through and knocks the power off. So for a second, the electronic clock resets to 12. But this candidate candidate one was so well prepared, she set a battery alarm back up. So there'd be no problem except a roommate changed the batteries and a video game remote and put the dead batteries back where the good ones were supposed to go. So the backup alarm didn't work either. She missed the interview. Canada too got drunk watching narcos and fell asleep on the couch without setting any alarms. They was late. They're both late. There's no question about it. Are they late for the same reason? Depends on what you say the reason is. Are they late because they were not on time? And that's a pretty good definition of late. Sure, but there's another way of looking at it. One candidate did everything we would want a conscientious employee to do and the other did not. Would you treat them the same? Would you be even in your treatment of them being late? Yes, we want to treat people the same. That is fairness. But good luck finding identical fact patterns where you could actually do that. I'm Trey Gaudy and we'll have more coming up.",
