    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89202.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "So again, what does it mean to be fair? Is it even handed? Is it that you get to do what I get to do? Well, the criminal justice system is designed to produce a fair result, but it certainly is not even handed. One party has the burden of proof, the other does not. One party may get more jury strikes than the other party gets. One party has appellate rights, the other does not have. One party gets to go last in closing argument, which is considered a big advantage. So the goal is fairness, but the process is not even handed. Is fairness balanced? I wish I had a nickel for every reporter who told me they thought their story was fair, even though I did not think their story was fair, and the word they would almost invariably use was balanced, that their story was balanced, that somehow balance is tantamount to fairness. Well, it can't be, can it? Because one person may utter a totally false statement, and another person utters the opposite false statement. The story is balanced. You have accurately quoted two wrong people. I don't know anyone who considers that to be fair. It's balanced for sure. You balance one misstatement with another misstatement. Great. You're balanced, but you're not fair. It's fairness accuracy. Well, that has to be part of it, but it has to be more than just simply being accurate. I remember Hurricane Dorian and the president tweeting that the storm might impact a handful of states, including Alabama. Okay, Alabama was not at risk. It's accurate to report the president included Alabama in his list of states. It's accurate to report Alabama was not at risk. But is it fair to leave out of your story the very important fact that he also included other states which actually were at risk? In other words, if the president warns residents of Georgia, South Carolina, North Carolina, and Alabama of a storm, is it fair to write a story belittling the president for including Alabama? Or is it fair to say the president was right on three and wrong on one? So something can be accurate and still not fair until we as a society settle on a working definition of fairness that we can all subscribe to. then that word will continue to be illusory, something we claim to care about but not enough to actually define or apply to others or most importantly apply to ourselves. So do you value fairness? That's the question. Do you value fairness? Do you reward it even when it hurts your own position? Do we as a culture value it? Can we define it? To whom do we go if we are looking for a fair judgment, a fair outcome? I want you to think about it. I want you to let me know how you define fairness. And once we have a working definition for it, something we can all universally subscribe to, then I want us to talk about the second part of this trilogy, which is something each of us has witnessed and experienced and dealt with and lived through, which is that miserable soul changing experience of being treated unfairly. and the insidious impact it has on us individually and has on groups that have been treated unfairly. Until next time, I want you to take care and let's all try to define fairness, but even more importantly, try to put it in practice. Thank you for listening to the Trey Yachty Podcast. I hope you continue to join me on Tuesdays and please rate and review this podcast on Apple Podcast or by going to foxnewspodcast.com. Let us know what you think. We want to hear from you. I'm Trey Gaudy with the Fox News Podcast Network.",
